a $A$ and the circumference $C$ of the circle. Since $C=2\\pi r$ and $A=\\pi {r}^{2},$ the relationship between $A$ and $C$ is\n\n$$\nA=\\pi {r}^{2}=\\pi {(\\frac{C}{2\\pi })}^{2}=\\frac{{C}^{2}}{4\\pi }.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{C}^{2}}{4\\pi }) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}C}(\\frac{{C}^{2}}{4\\pi }) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{C}{2\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen $r=5\\,\\text{cm},$ $C=2\\pi (5)=10\\pi \\text{ cm}.$ Hence, substituting $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=-8\\pi $ and $C=10\\pi $, and solving for $\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-8\\pi  & =\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{10\\pi }{2\\pi } \\\\ -8\\pi  & =\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 5 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{8\\pi }{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the circumference is decreasing at a rate of $\\frac{8\\pi }{5}\\text{ cm/s}.$" 13194139533466 17592186045585 true] [17592186395804 1075 #edb/ref 17592186395805 13194139533466 17592186045585 true] [17592186395804 1075 #edb/ref 17592186395806 13194139533466 17592186045585 true] [17592186395804 1075 #edb/ref 17592186395807 13194139533466 17592186045585 true] [17592186395804 1075 #edb/ref 17592186395808 13194139533466 17592186045585 true] [17592186395804 1075 #edb/ref 17592186395809 13194139533466 17592186045585 true] [17592186395804 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533466 17592186045585 true] [17592186395804 1077 "selection" 13194139533466 17592186045585 true] [17592186395804 1079 #edb/ref 17592186395806 13194139533466 17592186045585 true] [17592186395804 1080 #uuid "ec61e892-1bbe-5a6b-9b14-17fd1b7ef765" 13194139533466 17592186045585 true] [17592186395805 1081 "\\,8\\pi \\,\\tex